      "page_idx": 16,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.736,
            0.047,
            0.847,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Number Play"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.087,
            0.848,
            0.145
          ],
          "content": "In the calculations below, digits are replaced by letters. Each letter stands for a particular digit (0 – 9). You have to figure out which digit each letter stands for."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.446,
            0.157,
            0.553,
            0.255
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.266,
            0.848,
            0.324
          ],
          "content": "Here, we have a one-digit number that, when added to itself twice, gives a 2-digit sum. The units digit of the sum is the same as the single digit being added."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.332,
            0.628,
            0.354
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "What could U and T be? Can T be 2? Can it be 3?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.362,
            0.68,
            0.382
          ],
          "content": "Once you explore, you will see that T = 5 and UT = 15."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.739,
            0.339,
            0.846,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.382,
            0.848,
            0.474
          ],
          "content": "Let us look at one more example shown on the right. HMM Here K2 means that the number is a 2-digit number having the digit ‘2’ in the units place and ‘K’ in the tens place. K2 is added to itself to give a 3-digit sum HMM. What digit should the letter M correspond to?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.473,
            0.848,
            0.512
          ],
          "content": "Both the tens place and the units place of the sum have the same digit."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.52,
            0.544,
            0.542
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "What about H? Can it be 2? Can it be 3?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.551,
            0.848,
            0.607
          ],
          "content": "These types of questions can be interesting and fun to solve! Here are some more questions like this for you to try out. Find out what each letter stands for."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.607,
            0.847,
            0.646
          ],
          "content": "Share how you thought about each question with your classmates; you may find some new approaches."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.655,
            0.812,
            0.73
          ],
          "content": "| YY Z 十 | B5 KP |  | C1 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 3D + ZOO ED5 | KP + PRR | + | C 1FF |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_16_table_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.74,
            0.835,
            0.761
          ],
          "content": "These types of questions are called ‘cryptarithms’ or ‘alphametics’."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.767,
            0.294,
            0.791
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.16,
            0.794,
            0.847,
            0.832
          ],
          "content": "1. A light bulb is ON. Dorjee toggles its switch 77 times. Will the bulb be on or off? Why?"
        },
